# Her own systemd, and why this file has to exist.
#
# THE SYMPTOM: no sound. Nothing else. The desktop came up, the wifi
# worked, the browser opened, and there was no volume to set and
# nothing came out of the speakers.
#
# THE CAUSE, in five steps, every one of them correct on its own:
#
#   1. build/forge expires the account's password on purpose, so that
#      a shipped image carries no usable credential. The account's
#      shadow entry reads lastchg=0.
#   2. logind starts user@<uid>.service to run her own systemd, which
#      is what starts pipewire and wireplumber.
#   3. user@.service has PAMName=systemd-user, and the stack the
#      distribution ships for it begins @include common-account.
#   4. common-account is pam_unix with new_authtok_reqd=done, and
#      pam_unix returns PAM_NEW_AUTHTOK_REQD for lastchg=0.
#   5. systemd calls pam_acct_mgmt() before opening the session and
#      treats a failure as fatal: 224/PAM.
#
# So there is no user systemd, so there is no pipewire, so there is no
# sound -- and nothing anywhere says any of this. $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is
# still set and /run/user/<uid> still exists, because
# user-runtime-dir@.service has no PAM stack at all and succeeds, so
# even the shell's own "no runtime directory" warning stays quiet.
#
# HOW IT WAS MISSED ONCE. An adversarial review found this by reading
# the five files above. It was then "disproved" on a booted machine
# that showed user@1000.service active -- because the person checking
# had logged in over the serial console first, been made to change the
# password, and thereby cleared the very expiry that causes it. The
# test created the condition that hid the bug. A later boot, checked
# before logging in, failed with 224/PAM as predicted.
#
# WHY THIS AND NOT SOMETHING ELSE. Nothing in this product ever shows
# a password prompt, so "the password must be changed" cannot be
# communicated or acted upon by the person using the machine. The
# expiry exists to stop a shipped image carrying a working credential,
# and it does that job at the places where a credential is actually
# used -- login, su, sudo, where a technician IS present and the
# forced change is the intended first-run flow. Starting her own
# systemd is not one of those places. It authenticates nobody; systemd
# has already decided who runs it.
#
# The session lines below are the distribution's, unchanged.
account required pam_permit.so

session  required pam_selinux.so close
session  required pam_selinux.so nottys open
session  required pam_loginuid.so
session  required pam_limits.so
@include common-session-noninteractive
session  optional pam_keyinit.so force revoke
session  optional pam_systemd.so
